[Music]  yup that's me charles foster kane you're  probably wondering how i got here here's  a newsreel to get you up to speed  [Music]  suddenly a man steps in the frame and  points a gun at her this is not her day  fade to black  title  three weeks earlier  i'm fascinated with a specific kind of  opening scene it's not exactly a cold  open since it doesn't always happen  before the title sequence and it's not  just a tangential scene that establishes  the tone of a movie like the beginning  of indiana jones it's a particular cult  open that drops you in the middle of the  story without any context we lost doug  what  we can't find doug  the opening is so well known that it's  even become its own meme popularized by  robot chicken in 2010.  whoa whoa whoa whoa whoa did you ever  have one of those moments where you  think how the hell did i get here since  this type of opening gets parodied so  often it might feel like a lazy  narrative choice i'm not a huge fan  personally of the whole three weeks  earlier teaser thing i feel like you  know we should start our stories where  they begin not start them where they get  interested but this supposed cliche is  also used in many critically acclaimed  stories from iconic tv dramas to  cinema's all-time classics even  ratatouille one of my favorite movies  replicates that classic meme almost  exactly  this is me so how can a seemingly tired  cliche also show up in so many stories  we love well it all started just a few  short millennia ago  movies and tv were not the first to skip  over parts of their stories the ancient  poem the odyssey begins at the end of  the trojan war so the reader starts  during odysseus's journey back home the  roman poet horus the video essayist of  his time described this choice as in  medius res translated to in the midst of  things making this kind of opening one  of the earliest identified tropes in  history and it seems to have caught on  like when hamlet starts after the king's  death skipping a core event and instead  starting with the appearance of the king  as a ghost so the opening i'm talking  about is a version of this ancient in  media's res we begin in the middle and  then rewind to show how the character  got there at its most basic this how i  got here trope adds mystery to a story  in many classic noir novels and movies  we start off seeing the character in a  crisis and it draws us into the plot  there's something exciting about  watching someone plan their heist  knowing that they will fail but not  knowing when or how in the first scene  of citizen kane we get a serious version  of this how i got here opening we  watched the death of the main character  charles foster kane the mystery of his  dying word rosebud drives the plot of  the story as we find out how he ended up  in a giant mansion all alone on the  comedic side a lot of animated movies  used this opening for laughs because of  the cold open in the emperor's new  groove we already know that cusco turns  into a llama so it's that much funnier  when he has his slow transformation at  the dinner table whether it's dramatic  or comedic the trope is a great way to  sprinkle irony into your story right  from the beginning we know something the  characters don't so talk to me about  vegas uh you know it should be pretty  mellow so when a cold open doesn't  provide that sense of irony it can feel  lazy the movie daredevil opens with him  falling on the floor seemingly close to  death but once we actually catch up to  that moment he just gets up and keeps  fighting not only does the cold open end  up feeling inconsequential but there's  also nothing really surprising about a  superhero getting a little beat up so  it's not much of a memorable hook to  begin with just showing something  climactic like someone near death or a  gun in their face isn't inherently  enough to make for a good how i got here  opening the moment should tease at  future narrative or character-driven  payoffs and this kind of opening is  often not necessary to begin with in  fact many movies work much better  without it mad max fury road starts with  a simple exposition then shows the rest  of the story in sequence that choice  lets the audience feel like max himself  constantly trapped in a never-ending  present danger with only split-second  flashbacks of past trauma although a mad  max cold open would be pretty funny  you see that guy right there that's me  you're probably wondering how i got into  this mess  jokes like that have become big on the  internet the formula is simple show a  precarious situation and add that famous  narration but the idea of telling  stories in this fashion extends far  beyond simple memes not only do many  movies and tv shows use this concept but  cold opens even get applied in areas  outside of media a research article  published in 2018 discusses applying the  cold's open to education it's the same  basic idea as starting a story in the  middle before the actual lesson teachers  first present a fresh problem to  students without any context and let  them try to work through it on their own  this technique helps introduce new ideas  because it forces the students to apply  existing knowledge and build on their  understanding as they enter the actual  lesson they can then better engage with  the material because they've already  established that introductory foundation  and they know what to look out for at  its core that's exactly how the how i  got here opening works in movies memes  or even dumb video essays the technique  forces the viewer to see something out  of context and create their own  associations with that new material by  forcing the viewer to build those  associations themselves when done well  cult opens make us more invested in  future character or narrative arcs and  they make the climactic payoffs more  emotionally satisfying that's why the  rosebud reveal in citizen kane ended up  so iconic and it's why the initial image  of walter white in his tidy whities made  his transformation into a gangster that  much more exciting the record scratch  meme pops up on the internet all the  time so the trope may feel inherently  cliche or lazy but i don't think that's  true at all we've craved the mystery and  excitement of the cult open for as long  as storytelling has existed and some of  the best movies in history use the  technique i think the trope has lasted  so long because it speaks to some innate  human curiosity we find joy in seeing  things without context moments that  force us to put the pieces together  ourselves whether that puzzle is an  iconic story or a quick joke so yeah  that's me praising stupid online memes  and comparing them to the intro of one  of the greatest movies of all time and  yes i am also wondering how i got here  if you liked the video hit the subscribe  button below and thanks to all my  patreon supporters for supporting this  video thanks for watching

